  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-2df08f3e Web logs overview\"**\n- ID: `eff-2df08f3e-web-logs-overview-f708343d637f`\n- Four panels, all on the Kibana Sample Data Logs data view:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Average response bytes (average of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (date histogram on `timestamp`)\n  - Requests by response code (terms on `response.keyword`, top 10)\n- Time range is last 1 year. I chose that because I couldn't confirm when the sample data was installed, so a shorter window might show nothing.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-2df08f3e 5xx spike\"**\n- ID: `419f7153-8768-4c2e-bc43-a3f4a7c5a3cf`\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), consumer `stackAlerts`, runs every 1m.\n- Query: `response.keyword` between `500` and `599`, over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Enabled, status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- **I enabled the rule.** You asked for something that \"checks every minute\", so I started it. It has no action connectors, so it will record alerts but won't notify anyone.\n- **I added the tag `EFF-2df08f3e`** to the rule to make it easy to find.\n- **5xx matching uses a string range** on `response.keyword`. This works because HTTP status codes are three digits. I didn't confirm it against real data, because I couldn't query Elasticsearch: the ES|QL endpoints returned 404, the Console proxy is disabled, and port 9200 refused connections. So the exploration came from the data view's field list, not from the actual values.",
